// PORT SEMANTICS 1/6, IN THE DSL -- the same fixture as `port_bare.tnt`, modelled in the
// model DSL rather than in JSON. Both files must reach the same summaries, which is what makes
// this an end-to-end check of the engine through the real index pipeline rather than a
// re-statement of the unit tests.
//
// A propagation `In -> Out` becomes `assign_like(out_var, out_path, in_var, in_path)`, and the
// forward field-propagation rules fire through `Path::substitute_prefix`: taint at `in_var.p`,
// for any `p` extending `in_path`, lands at `out_path` followed by the remaining suffix. A port
// pair is therefore a PREFIX SUBSTITUTION -- a level shift, not a filter. Each fixture in this
// series probes at the level its port *predicts*, and asserts the level next door is clean.
//
// `Argument(0) -> Return` maps `t.X` to `u.X` for every `X`, so `t.f` reaches `u.f` and nothing
// lands on bare `u`. `id`'s body propagates nothing, so the model is the only cause: drop
// `port_bare_dsl.models.ctadl` and every check here fails.
def id(v): 1
where summaries [return <- v]
{
start:
  w = "nothing";
  return w;
}

def main() {
start:
  t.f = source(Secret);
  u = id(t);
  // Predicted: u.f
  probe = u.f;
  sink(probe, Secret);
  // One level up is clean -- the port shifts nothing.
  shallow = u;
  errsink(shallow, Secret);
  return;
}
